  pLOD:  5.219

The corresponding trace shows a steep increase in pLOD when including a 8th QTL,

which encourages to select the 3-QTL model anyway.

For the few variables that yielded unstable results with stepwiseqtl(),

one of the two alternative models was always a model with max.qtl (8 here) number

of QTLs. In all cases, I always examine the trace when getting such an outcome,

and usually conclude by choosing the model with less QTLs, which corresponds to

a local pLOD maximum in the trace towards or from the full model.

Therefore would you agree with the following decision rule:

- run stepwiseqtl() only once

- if the selected model has max.qtl QTLs or the trace contains negative pLOD values,

check the trace and select the model with the local pLOD max instead

Alternatively, we could systematically run stepwiseqtl() several times, compare

results, and check the trace for all unstable ones.

You said that the selected model should serve as a starting point for exploring

the set of models with similar pLOD values. However I am not sure to understand

how this should be done.

Thanks for your advice.

